# JSONL fixtures are byte-exact by design and must survive checkout untouched.
#
# Two things would otherwise silently break them, and both would turn a passing
# test into a test that cannot fail:
#
#   - CRLF conversion rewrites the 0x0A framing the adapters split on
#     (docs/design.md §4), and would append a terminator to the deliberately
#     torn final record in every fixture — the record that pins "a trailing
#     partial line is not a record".
#   - Text normalization can rewrite the raw U+2028 embedded in each fixture,
#     which is the character the framing rule exists to survive.
#
# internal/jsonl and both adapter test suites assert those bytes directly, so a
# rewrite fails the build rather than passing quietly. This entry is the belt.
*.jsonl -text -diff

# Golden renders are compared byte for byte at fixed terminal widths. Render
# emits \n, so a CRLF checkout makes every golden differ by one byte per line —
# on Windows, which is the reference environment AND where CI runs.
internal/hud/testdata/golden/*.txt -text
internal/council/testdata/golden/*.txt -text

# README.md's hero pictures are goldens too, and for the same reason: they are
# emitted from those renders (internal/svgframe) and compared byte for byte
# against the committed file, so a CRLF checkout would make every one of them
# differ on the platform CI runs on.
images/*.svg -text

# SQLite fixtures are binary: page headers, b-tree pointers and WAL checksums.
# A single byte rewritten in transit fails a checksum, and a checkpoint's worth
# of frames would be silently dropped by the very reader under test.
*.db binary
*.db-wal binary

# Same files, Cursor's extension for them. The `-wal` entry matters more here
# than anywhere else in the repo: one fixture's main file is a single empty
# page and every byte of its content is in the sidecar (decisions/007), so a
# rewritten sidecar is not a degraded fixture, it is an empty one.
*.vscdb binary
*.vscdb-wal binary
